  "query_preview": "..\n\nSaturday through Sunday: A low pressure system moving into the \nforecast area will keep the weekend active, bringing continued gusty \nwinds, rain showers, mountain snow, and colder than normal \ntemperatures. This low is the second and stronger of the two lows \nthat have moved through this weekend. Continued westerly flow across \nthe Cascades will keep winds elevated, with gusts 20-25 mph in the \nafternoon throughout the basin, and isolated areas of 30-35 mph \ngusts in the Cascade gaps. In areas with stronger wind gusts, \nimpacts such as blowing unsecured objects and small tree limbs \nalongside difficult driving conditions for high profile vehicles \ncould be seen.\n\nThis low will bring precipitation to the area, of which the bulk \nwill fall through late this morning. Lingering showers will last \nacross the eastern third of Washington and the Idaho Panhandle \nthrough Sunday night. Areas outside the mountains will see less than \n0.10 inches of rain in total primarily due to rain shadowing, \nespecially across the deeper Basin. This will be a colder system, \nwith the low ushering in 850mb temperatures 5-10 degrees lower than \nnormal. This will result in mountain passes seeing a return of snow \nand associated travel impacts. Through Sunday, Stevens Pass has a \n30% chance of seeing 6 or more inches of snow, and as such there is \na Winter Weather Advisory valid through 11am this morning. Lookout \nPass will receive less, with a 60% chance of 2 inches or more of \nsnow. Much of this snow will fall Saturday. Travelers should prepare \nfor wintry travel conditions across mountain passes over the \nweekend. \n\nOvernight temperatures through Monday will be 5-10 degrees cooler \nthan normal, falling into the mid-30s. Given that growing season has \nstarted, frost impacts look likely, particularly Saturday night into \nSunday morning, when temperatures are expected to be the coldest. \nNBM probabilities show a 50% chance at seeing 36 degrees or lower \nfor the Western Okanogan County, Okanogan Highlands, Northeast \nMountains, Spokane, Coeur d'Alene, and Pullman areas. Frost \nheadlines are looking likely, as temperatures this low could harm \nsensitive outdoor vegetation. \n\nThere is a chance for thunderstorms each afternoon and evening \nthrough Sunday as well as the low passes through. Tonight, \nthunderstorms will largely be confined to the Cascades and northern \nmountains. Through the weekend, lowering temperatures aloft will\nincrease lapse rates. CAPE values of 300-500 J/kg will be seen \nover northeast Washington and the Idaho Panhandle, resulting in \na 20-30% chance of thunderstorms Saturday and Sunday evening. \nThere is a 10-20% chance for thunderstorms across much of the \nrest of the forecast area. \n\nMonday through Friday: Clusters and long term models are in decent \nagreement of a strengthening upper level ridge moving in as the low \npressure system tracks eastward. This will usher in temperatures \nwarming to near or slightly above normal and drying conditions. \nSpread in temperatures is low through Wednesday, but near the end of \nthe week it increases as model uncertainty grows. Clusters around \nthis time begin disagreeing on if the ridge will stay in place or a \nlow pressure system will dip down into the area, bringing cooler \ntemperatures and more chances for precip. /AS",
